 ## Operating characteristics
 
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per ray-box test, as it involves a fixed number of arithmetic operations (6 subtractions, 6 multiplications/additions, 6 comparisons) [S8, S53, S54, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$, requiring only a few scalar variables to store intermediate results [S8, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** The slab method is one of the fastest ray-box intersection algorithms due to its simplicity and lack of branching (when implemented branchlessly) [S51, S53, S54, unofficial]. Transformations to eliminate branches or reduce operations can yield speed enhancements of approximately 10-14% [S59, S60, unofficial].
-*   **Scaling:** Scales linearly with the number of rays, making it suitable for parallel execution on GPUs [G].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, fast, robust with proper handling of edge cases, and easily parallelizable [S8, S51, S53, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Only works for axis-aligned boxes. For oriented boxes, a transformation is required [S1, unofficial][S58][S60, unofficial]. Numerical precision issues can arise with rays parallel to axes or near box boundaries [S2, S18, unofficial][S58][S63, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** When intersecting rays with axis-aligned volumes or bounding volumes in acceleration structures (e.g., BVH, Octree) [S1, S10, S44, unofficial].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When dealing with oriented bounding boxes (use [Ray-OBB Intersection Test](6603-ray-obb-intersection-test.md)) or when higher precision is required for degenerate cases (consider [Exact Rational Ray-Surfel Evaluation](2915-exact-rational-ray-surfel-evaluation.md)).
+*   **Time Complexity**: $O(1)$ per ray-box test, involving a constant number of arithmetic operations (6 subtractions, 6 multiplications, 6 comparisons, 3 max/min operations) [S8, S58, S64, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity**: $O(1)$ auxiliary space [S8, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance**: The slab method is highly efficient on modern GPUs due to its branchless nature (when implemented with min/max functions) and low instruction count [S55, S67, unofficial]. Transformations to reduce branch mispredictions can achieve speed enhancements of approximately 10-14% depending on the compiler [S63, S64, unofficial].
+*   **Scaling**: Performance scales linearly with the number of rays, making it suitable for parallel execution on GPUs where each thread processes one or more rays [G].
+*   **Strengths**: Simple to implement, numerically stable with proper epsilon handling, highly parallelizable, and fast [S8, S55, unofficial][S62].
+*   **Limitations**: Only works for axis-aligned boxes. Requires precomputation of inverse direction. Degenerate cases (zero direction components) require special handling [S2, S21, unofficial][S62].
+*   **When to Use**: When the volume or object is axis-aligned, and fast, exact intersection tests are required for ray traversal or culling [S1, unofficial][S62].
+*   **When Not to Use**: When the box is rotated (use [Ray-OBB Intersection Test](6603-ray-obb-intersection-test.md)) or when hardware rasterization is preferred for setup data generation ([Ray-Box Intersection via OpenGL Geometry Shaders](1768-ray-box-intersection-via-opengl-geometry-shaders.md)) [S1, unofficial][S62].
 
 ## Background
 
-The slab method for ray-box intersection is a classic algorithm in computer graphics, often attributed to Kay and Kajiya (1986) [S5, S10, unofficial]. It treats the AABB as the intersection of three slabs (pairs of parallel planes) and computes the intersection interval for each slab, then intersects these intervals to find the overall entry and exit points [S51, unofficial][S52][S53, S54, unofficial]. The method is widely used in volume rendering, ray tracing, and collision detection due to its efficiency and simplicity [S1, S8, S51, unofficial]. Variations and optimizations, such as branchless implementations and axis-normalized transformations, have been developed to further improve performance [S51, S53, S59, S60, unofficial].
+The slab method for ray-AABB intersection is a classic algorithm in computer graphics, often attributed to Kay and Kajiya (1986) [S5, unofficial]. It is widely used in volume rendering, ray tracing, and spatial partitioning (e.g., octrees, BVHs) to determine the entry and exit points of rays into bounding volumes [S1, S5, S10, unofficial][S53]. The method treats the AABB as the intersection of three pairs of parallel planes (slabs) and computes the intersection interval by clipping the ray against each slab [S11, S52, S55, S58, unofficial]. Variations and optimizations, such as branchless SIMD implementations and axis-normalized transformations, have been developed to improve performance on modern hardware [S55, S63, S64, unofficial].
 
